Verdict: MAJOR

Retrieval adequacy: adequate

Strengths
- The draft is better bounded against current calibration surfaces: `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py`, `src/worldenergydata/cost/calibration/cost_predictor.py`, `tests/unit/cost/test_cost_predictor.py`, `tests/unit/cost/test_proxy_comparison.py`, and `tests/unit/cost/test_calibration_schema.py` are the real repo boundaries.
- The v1 policy is now deterministic in-plan instead of deferred.
- The plan no longer tries to fold linkage or analytics into this child issue.

Findings by severity

critical
- `#336` is still not grounded to the current repo state because its required input surface does not exist yet. Current code only has the sanctioned-project `CostDataPoint` schema in `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py` and the USD-only sanctioned dataset loader in `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py`. The fields this plan says it will preserve (`amount`, `currency`, `magnitude_scale`, `fiscal_year`, `as_reported_metric_name`) are not present anywhere in current code. Parent `#334` is still a plan, not landed code. That makes the acceptance criteria untestable against present repo surfaces and is still a blocker for moving this plan onward unchanged.

high
- The output contract is underspecified and internally inconsistent with the file list. The pseudocode says `normalize_disclosure_money(record, ...)` returns `record + normalized fields`, but the plan only creates `disclosure_normalization.py` and `fx_policy_v1.py`. It does not name any schema/result model file to define those additive normalized fields. If `#334` lands with typed disclosure records, this issue will either have to modify sibling-owned schema files anyway or fall back to untyped dicts. That is a real design hole.
- Several proposed tests require disclosure fixtures that this issue does not own and the repo does not yet have. `test_same_currency_rows_use_no_fx_but_still_record_method`, `test_cross_currency_rows_can_be_compared_when_policy_inputs_exist`, and `test_scale_is_applied_before_normalization` all assume a seeded disclosure corpus with at least one USD row, one non-USD row, `magnitude_scale`, and `fiscal_year`. The plan does not say whether those fixtures come from `#334`, local test-only rows, or a normalization-owned dataset stub.

medium
- The plan says unit is preserved unchanged, but the test list never covers missing/invalid `unit` behavior or asserts unit preservation on non-comparable rows.
- The deterministic policy is numeric, but the tests are mostly structural. There is no explicit golden-value test for a known FX/year conversion and no direct test for both rebasing directions around base year 2020.
- `src/worldenergydata/cost/calibration/cost_predictor.py` already contains a private 2020 / 3% rebasing policy for sanctioned data. This draft does not state whether `#336` must intentionally duplicate that policy, centralize it, or stay isolated. Leaving that implicit invites drift.

low
- `tests/unit/cost/test_calibration_schema.py` is a weak regression boundary for this issue because it only proves current sanction-schema behavior, not disclosure normalization isolation. It is not wrong to cite it, just low-signal.

Missing tests
- Raw-field preservation for non-comparable rows, not just comparable rows.
- Exact expected normalized numeric output for at least one fixed FX/year example.
- Base-year adjustment in both directions relative to 2020.
- Missing or invalid `unit` handling.
- Consistent `comparability_status` plus `None` normalized fields serialization for all not-comparable cases.

Scope creep concerns
- If this issue has to introduce or change disclosure record types to carry normalized fields, it is no longer “normalization only”; it starts pulling schema ownership back from `#334`.
- If this issue has to seed cross-currency disclosure examples itself to make tests pass, it is starting to absorb dataset work that belongs with the disclosure layer.

Weakest assumption
- That `#334` will land with a disclosure record shape that can accept additive normalized fields without requiring further schema/file changes outside the two new files named here.

Most likely implementation failure mode
- `disclosure_normalization.py` ends up returning ad hoc dicts because no typed normalized disclosure result exists, and later code/tests diverge on field names and null/status semantics.

Most likely test gap
- Happy-path comparable rows pass, but not-comparable rows silently lose raw metadata or produce inconsistent status/`None` fields because failure-path preservation is not explicitly tested.

Future issues suggested
- A dedicated typed normalized-disclosure result/schema issue if `#334` does not already provide extension points.
- A policy-centralization issue for shared rebasing constants/method naming between disclosure normalization and `cost_predictor.py`.
- A fixture/data-maintenance issue for versioned in-repo FX tables and disclosure normalization examples.

Review confidence
- High
